 "derivation":"Two words, two verdicts. (a) δαμάω / δάμνημι, aorist ἐδάμασ(σ)α 'to tame, subdue, break in', with δμητός 'tamed' (< *dm̥h₂-tos), δμήτωρ 'tamer', δάμαλις 'heifer', πανδαμάτωρ 'all-subduer', is the regular Greek continuant of the zero-grade *dmh₂- (Pokorny 199-200 lists the whole family; Greek, he notes, has no o-grade forms). This half is confirmed. (b) ἀδάμας, gen. ἀδάμαντος, is attested from Hesiod on for 'the hardest metal, probably steel', then for a hard bright stone and finally for the diamond; the traditional analysis, which Watkins and Klein both give and which Nourai copies, is ἀ- privative + δαμ- = 'unconquerable'. Modern Greek etymology no longer accepts that: Beekes calls the semantics strange and takes ἀδάμας for a Semitic/oriental loan reshaped by folk etymology on δαμάω, comparing Akkadian elmēšu 'a precious stone' — and Wiktionary's Middle Persian entry says the same in the other direction ('usually derived from Byzantine Greek ἀδάμας, but the Greek word itself is now considered an oriental loanword'). So the node's own internal derivation ('a-damas: unconquerable') is the 19th/20th-century handbook view that the current standard reference work rejects; the word itself and its 'hard stone' meaning are correctly given, which is why the node is graded plausible rather than disputed.",
